Revision history for App-Clod

0.002 (not yet released)
    - Install the system packages that the spokes declare.  A spoke names
      them under x_system_packages in its metamerge.yml, with programs that
      must run once they are installed.  With system_packages in clod.conf,
      the harness scan reads the declaration from the default branch of each
      spoke, the self check asks the packager of the host which of them it
      lacks and runs the programs, and the dashboard shows each spoke that
      needs packages, with an Install packages button.  The button queues
      packages_install, which runs the installer of the configuration with
      sudo and checks again when it is done.  A declaration that is not
      valid is a finding of its spoke.  Needs Dist::Conformance 0.003.
    - Filter the action items of the dashboard by type.  Three buttons in
      the header, Failures, Warnings and Info, each switch one level on or
      off, and all are on by default.  A person could not hide the harness
      warnings to see only the failures.
    - Add the autovivification pragma as a runtime requirement. The modules
      that read nested data now say "no autovivification", so that a read of
      a missing key does not create it. Variables::RequireNoAutovivification
      asks for this. Paired hash reads are now slices, and the retry loop in
      triage has a named loop variable.
    - Show two cards at the top of the Overview tab of the health page: the
      open issues that nobody is assigned to, with the issues opened in the
      last 7 days under it, and the pull requests merged in the last 7 days.
      A new task, activity_sweep, counts them every fifteen minutes over the
      hub and every spoke.  The cards are now four to a row, so that eight
      of them lay out evenly.
    - Keep the tools of the agent in agent_lib, a local lib of the service
      user that the agent may read and not change, when clod.conf names
      one: Perl::Critic, each policy of the critic profiles of the
      standards, Perl::Tidy and Devel::NYTProf.  Every claude run gets it
      first on PERL5LIB and PATH.  clod deps install fills it, and the
      dashboard installs what it lacks and upgrades what is outdated, so a
      policy that the standards add reaches the agent with no provision.
      Without an agent_lib, claude gets no PERL5LIB, so not the modules of
      the bot either.
    - Refresh the references of the spokes once after each spoke sweep, in
      refresh_references, and let a triage or a reply refresh only the one
      of its request.  Each of them refreshed every spoke, so two at once
      cloned and fetched the same repositories, and one failed with
      "invalid reference: origin/master".
    - Take a lock of the bare clone of a spoke, with Mutex::Flock, while one
      process clones or fetches it, set its fetch refspec on every fetch,
      and make a missing reference again after git forgets its worktree.
      The builtin flock is banned in .preferred_modules.ini in favor of
      Mutex::Flock, which lets go of the lock when the code under it dies.
    - Allow a run that changes a spoke perlcritic, perltidy, make,
      nytprofhtml and nytprofmerge by default.  The hooks criticize and
      tidy only what a commit stages, so a change to the profile could not
      be checked against the whole tree, and a profile could be made with
      perl -d:NYTProf but not read.
    - A spoke whose harness fix waits for review says so on the dashboard,
      with a link to the pull request, and offers no second Fix.  The
      harness scan records that pull request while it is open.  The item
      offered Fix again as soon as the job finished.
    - Assign each pull request that the hub opens, so that it reaches
      somebody's notifications: a fix to its requester, and a harness fix,
      with its issue, to who pressed the button.  A login that is not in
      authorized_users gives every authorized user.  No pull request of the
      hub had an assignee, and one from the dashboard reached nobody.
    - Draw a button of an action item in the color of the text of its item.
      It was dark on the dark tint of an item, and could hardly be seen.
    - Keep the Perl modules of the bot in local_lib, a local lib that the
      service user owns, when clod.conf names one, so the bot can upgrade
      them with no sudo.  clod deps install|outdated|upgrade works on it
      from the shell.  An update installs what the new commit needs there
      before it restarts, and the dashboard says when a module has a newer
      release on CPAN, with an Upgrade modules button that upgrades it and
      restarts the bot.
    - Say on the dashboard when the bot is out of date, with an Update
      button that pulls and restarts the units of restart_units, its own
      unit last.  And when a plugin of the account that claude runs as is
      behind its marketplace, with an Update plugins button.  self_check
      looks every half hour.  The header shows the short sha that runs.
    - Compare a version with two parts as dotted, so 0.10 is newer than
      0.9.  The marketplace check took 0.10 for older than 0.9.
    - Buttons on the action items of the dashboard: Retry and Ignore on a
      failed job, and Fix on a spoke that fails harness checks.  Fix
      queues harness_fix, which runs the update of the harness standards
      on a new branch, with no claude run, files an issue, and opens a
      pull request that closes it.  Every button posts to /act, which
      takes a request only with the header X-Clod-Action, so another site
      cannot press one.
    - Keep the work of a fix or an answer to a review whose push fails.
      The commits stay in the bare clone under refs/clod/kept/<job>, and
      the answer of the run in the notes of the job, so a retry of the job
      pushes them and opens the pull request or replies, with no second
      claude run.  An answer to a review drops what it kept if the branch
      moved since, so that its push does not undo someone else's commits.
    - Try again when GitHub fails for a moment.  A git clone, fetch or push
      that ends in a server error or a lost connection gets up to four
      tries, five, thirty and a hundred and twenty seconds apart, and so
      does an API GET.  A request that changes something is tried again
      only after a 502, 503 or 504.  A push that GitHub took but answered
      with a server error counts as done.  A push that failed with a 500
      threw away a finished rebase.
    - Tell the run that answers a review not to push after a rebase.  It
      tried, which failed, because the account it runs as has no token.
    - Tell an answer to a review the base branch of its pull request.  A
      request to rebase that names no branch goes onto that branch, and a
      rewrite of the branch gets conflicts resolved on both sides and the
      tests run, because no pre-commit hook checks it.  The sort writes
      out what a short request, such as "rebase", means.
    - Sort what waits on the bot's own pull request before changing it.  A
      run that can only read answers each question in its thread, and only
      a request for a change goes on to a run that can commit.  A mention on
      that pull request is sorted as well.  A question in a line comment
      went to the run that changes code, and its prompt asked for a change
      or a reason not to make one.
    - A reply that finds a request for a change on the pull request of
      someone else starts a fix.  It started work on the review comments,
      which can push only to a pull request of the bot, and failed.
    - Find who wrote a review on a pull request from its latest review or
      line comment, when the notification names no latest comment, or
      names the pull request itself.  A review of an authorized user was
      ignored, as asked for by nobody known.
    - An assignment to a pull request becomes work on the review comments
      if the pull request is the bot's own, and a review if it is not.  It
      was a fix, which took the pull request for an issue and opened a
      second pull request with the same change.
    - Make no job for work on the review comments when no review comment
      or comment waits for an answer.  A triage ran first and then found
      nothing to do.
    - Drop a repository of spokes that is not public.  The spoke sweep now
      asks GitHub for the visibility of each one, spokes() leaves out those
      that it dropped, and the Overview tab names each with its reason.
      Nothing kept a private repository out of a public installation before.
    - Fail a fix or an answer to a review that made no commit because
      claude refused some of its calls, and name each refused call in the
      error.  Such a run finished as no change before, with a reply that
      said the change was made.
    - With run_as, the account that claude runs as brings its own Claude
      token.  sudo logged the token of the worker, which went through
      --preserve-env, with its value.
    - Allow each Bash permission rule for the command that rtk makes of it,
      so that an unattended run is not refused every git and prove.
    - Make a new repository when an authorized user asks for one, if
      create_repos names the organizations where the bot may, then work in it
      like any other spoke and open an issue for its first release.
    - File a follow-up issue in a spoke when an authorized user asks for one,
      assigned to them, with follow_up_max as the cap of one request.

0.001 2026-10-28 TEODESIAN
    - Initial Release
